The values that a data file does not hold, and the effect of each way of handling them on an estimate, form the subject of this module of the [companion curriculum](index.qmd). In the field experiment on which the Housing and racial bias case study rests, described in full on the [background page](../background.qmd), the original authors emailed public housing authorities across the United States from names meant to indicate a white, a Black or a Hispanic applicant and found that replies to the Hispanic names opened with the sender's name less often [@EinsteinGlick2017DoesRaceAffect]. The original authors' public file is unusually complete for a social-science dataset, yet it lacks three kinds of information, each missing for a different reason and each entering the analysis at a different point. Every number on the page is computed from the analysis file `data/housing_emails.csv` when the page is built, and the construction of that file is followed step by step in the [module on building the analysis file](data-management.qmd).

## 1. Three kinds of missing information {#sec-kinds}



@tbl-kinds presents the three kinds, together with the third in which each housing authority falls on the assessment score of the Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD). No specification on the curve uses this assessment third, but since it is missing far more often than anything else in the file, it illustrates what the choices examined in section 5 can do when much is at stake. The last column counts the rows affected by each kind of missing information in the workshop's reference specification, that is, among the coded replies to white and Hispanic names.


