-------------------------------------
         0 |    556 |      10 |  1.80
         1 |    458 |      13 |  2.84
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
stats::chisq.test(table(housing$group, is.na(housing$reply)))$p.value
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 0.1313879
```


:::
:::




The bounces are few and spread across the groups, at 2.8% of the emails with white names, 0.9% with Black names and 3.0% with Hispanic names, and a $\chi^2$ test finds no more difference between the groups than chance would produce (p = 0.131). For the name to have caused a bounce, a mail server would have had to refuse a message because of its sender. The counts by group give no reason to suspect such a refusal, but the counts by name are uneven, all the bounces among the white names having come from Emily and none from Brett, with none from Tyrone either. Chance would rarely produce such a spread across the six names (a $\chi^2$ test across the six names, with its p-value simulated because the counts are small, gives p = 0.002), and the sending account may thus have played a part. A bounced email is therefore arguably best read as an email to which the treatment was never delivered, for reasons that no official could have influenced. Leaving the bounced emails out, as every specification on the curve does, keeps the comparison among the delivered emails nearly as random as the assignment of the names made it.

The alternative, counting a bounced email as one that went unanswered, matters only when all emails are counted, since a bounced email is never a reply. Among all delivered emails to white and Hispanic names, with the uncoded replies counted as not named, the gap in greetings by name is −13.2 points. Counting the bounced emails as unanswered moves the gap to −12.8 points, a change that is small but produced by broken addresses rather than by officials, which is why the [specification menu](../menu.qmd#level-measurement) excludes the bounced emails from the curve.

## 4. Replies without a greeting code {#sec-uncoded}

The original authors coded the greeting of every reply but 5, and the file does not indicate why these five replies were left uncoded.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
housing |>
  datawizard::data_filter(reply == 1, is.na(named)) |>
  datawizard::data_select(c("email_id", "name", "group", "hard_email", "reply24"))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
# A tibble: 5 × 5
  email_id name     group    hard_email reply24
     <dbl> <chr>    <chr>         <dbl>   <dbl>
1      521 Tyrone   black             0       1
2      638 Shanice  black             1       1
3      808 Emily    white             1       1
4      833 Emily    white             0       1
5      950 Santiago hispanic          1       0
```


:::
:::


Three of the five uncoded replies went to white or Hispanic names, two to Emily and one to Santiago, and only these three can move the comparison stated in the claim. The specification menu offers two ways of handling the uncoded replies, the first of which, leaving them out, is what a model does by default and what four of the five analysts in the SCORE robustness study [@AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness] did. The second, counting the uncoded replies as replies that did not open with the name, is a defensible reading, since a reply in which the coders found no greeting probably did not begin with one, and analyst 25N5D made that choice, without stating it, by keeping these replies in the counts of emails. Because the uncoded replies arose after the officials had read the name, neither option can rely on random assignment, and the safest check is one that assumes nothing about why the codes are missing. Such a check is provided by the two extreme codings, one counting all three replies as named when the name was Hispanic and as not named when it was white, which moves the gap as far towards zero as these replies can move it, and the other doing the reverse.


::: {#tbl-uncoded .cell tbl-cap='The gap in greetings by name between Hispanic and white names among the replies, under four codings of the three uncoded replies to these names: left out, counted as not named, and the two extreme codings. Differences in proportions with HC2 standard errors. Source: `data/housing_emails.csv`.'}

```{.r .cell-code}
codings <- list(
  `left out` = datawizard::data_filter(replies_wh, !is.na(named)),
  `not named` = datawizard::data_modify(replies_wh, named = dplyr::coalesce(named, 0)),
  `named if Hispanic, not if white` = datawizard::data_modify(replies_wh, named = dplyr::coalesce(named, hisp)),
  `named if white, not if Hispanic` = datawizard::data_modify(replies_wh, named = dplyr::coalesce(named, 1 - hisp))
)
uncoded_gaps <- purrr::imap(codings, \(d, coding) {
  stats::lm(named ~ hisp, data = d) |>
    parameters::model_parameters(vcov = "HC2") |>
    dplyr::filter(Parameter == "hisp") |>
    dplyr::mutate(coding = coding, replies = nrow(d))
}) |>
  purrr::list_rbind()

uncoded_gaps |>
  datawizard::data_modify(gap = sub("^-", "−", sprintf("%.1f", 100 * Coefficient)),
                          se = sprintf("%.1f", 100 * SE)) |>
  datawizard::data_select(c(Coding = "coding", `Gap (points)` = "gap", SE = "se", Replies = "replies")) |>
  tinytable::tt() |>
  tinytable::style_tt(j = 2:4, align = "r")
```

