Every strategy that retains all 362 replies yields a gap close to the unadjusted −20.0 points, from −19.8 to −20.4, with standard errors of about 5.2 points. The complete-case analysis, by contrast, yields −17.2 points on 246 replies, with a larger standard error, and almost the whole of its departure is already present in the unadjusted gap among the same complete cases. While the adjustment itself appears to change little, dropping the authorities without a score changes the comparison and costs the precision that the adjustment was meant to buy.

Multiple imputation adds almost nothing here. The part of the variance in the gap attributable to the variation between the imputations, reported by mice as the fraction of missing information, is 0.009, because the name was assigned at random and thus carries no information about the missing scores, whatever these scores are. In an experiment the category for the missing yields the same answer without any model of the missing values, and it is on this ground that @ZhaoDing2024AdjustNotAdjust argue for preferring it. Imputation earns its place where the variable with missing values is itself the object of the analysis rather than an aid to precision. A comparison of the gap across the thirds of percent Hispanic, presented in the [subgroups module](subgroups.qmd), cannot give the replies without a third a category of their own, and an observational comparison, such as one among the officials who replied, has no random assignment to fall back on. In such analyses the observational case argued by @Allison2009MissingData and by @KingEtAl2001AnalyzingIncompletePolitical applies in full, and the plausibility of MAR, here a missing score explained by the size of the community and the other recorded columns, becomes the assumption on which the estimate depends.

## 6. Missing values on the curve {#sec-curve}

Each kind of missing information enters the specification curve of the case study in its own way. The bounced emails lie outside the experiment under every option, because no official read a bounced email. The handling of the uncoded replies is a decision on the menu, with these replies either left out or counted as not named, and the two extreme codings of section 4 show that no handling of the uncoded replies can change the conclusion. The community thirds are handled as complete cases, and the specifications adjusting for them therefore lose 5 of the 362 coded replies in the reference comparison. Part of the shift between the adjusted and the unadjusted specifications, as the [specification menu](../menu.qmd#level-estimation) notes, comes from the rows lost by the adjustment rather than from the adjustment itself. A declared analysis can name a different handling, such as a category for the missing, provided that the declaration names it before the model is run, since a choice made after the estimates are known is itself one of the forking paths discussed in the [multiverse module](multiverse.qmd).

